  "query_preview": "..\nWednesday: An amplified upper-level ridge extends from the \ndesert southwest into the Inland Northwest while a closed low \npressure system moves northward offshore the west coast. \nSoutherly flow aloft will maintain a steady stream of monsoonal \nmoisture into the region, resulting in periods of cloud cover. \n850 mb temperatures are forecast to climb to around 27-29C, \nsupporting widespread surface high temperatures in the upper 90s\nto lower 100s. There is uncertainty in the exact temperature \nforecast due to smoke from local fires in Washington and Oregon.\nLight winds will allow smoke to accumulate near active fires, \nlikely blocking afternoon solar radiation and locally limiting \nthe degree of warming compared to the current forecast.\n\nThursday through Saturday: A critical fire weather pattern will\nevolve as the offshore upper low approaches the Pacific \nNorthwest and a weak embedded shortwave moves through the \nregion. Continued monsoonal moisture advection will result in \nelevated instability (50- 100 J/KG of 700-300mb MUCAPE), \nbringing a 10-15% chance of thunderstorms as early as late \nWednesday night into Thursday morning across the central WA \nCascades. Instability transitions to surface based by Thursday \nafternoon, supporting a continued 12-25% chance of thunderstorms\nacross the Cascades into the Okanogan Highlands, far northeast \nWashington and north Idaho, southeast Washington, and the \nsouthern Idaho Panhandle. Very hot conditions will persist into \nThursday with model soundings showing DCAPE values exceeding \n1000 J/kg in the afternoon. This will create a favorable \nenvironment for strong, localized downdraft wind gusts with any \nshower or thunderstorm activity.\n\nBy Friday into Saturday, an upper-level trough digs off the \nPacific Northwest coast from the Gulf of Alaska, pushing the \ninitial offshore low eastward, driving a dry cold front across \nthe region. An increasing cross-Cascade pressure gradient will \nbring breezy winds through the Cascade gaps beginning Thursday, \nfollowed by widespread breezier conditions on Friday with better\nvertical mixing. The most impactful period is currently \nforecasted for Saturday as an 85-96 knot jet streak moves into \nthe Inland Northwest ahead of the upper-level trough. Stronger \nwinds at 850mb and 700mb (around 25 mph and 32 mph, \nrespectively) combined with a +8-10mb PDX- GEG surface pressure \ngradient will support widespread gusty winds with southwest wind\ngusts 25 to 40 MPH. On Friday the NBM indicates a 30-70% chance\nfrom the Okanogan Valley, Waterville Plateau, and the upper \nColumbia Basin into the West Plains for sustained wind speeds \nexceeding 15 mph combined with minimum relative humidity below \n20%. Some cooling is expected on Saturday, raising the afternoon\nrelative humidity, with a 40-60% chance for the same \ncombination across the Inland Northwest. Despite the wetter RHs,\nthe winds are expected to be stronger than Friday, negating any\nbenefit of the cooler temperatures. The timeline of very hot \ntemperatures, lightning potential, and subsequent dry, breezy \nfrontal passage presents a high impact fire weather scenario for\nthe Inland Northwest Thursday through Saturday.\n\nSunday through Tuesday: Ensembles are in broad agreement for \nthe next upper-level trough to move onshore on Sunday, bringing \ncooler conditions and a chance for light preciptiation along the\nCascade crest. Uncertainty in the synoptic pattern increases \nMonday and Tuesday with around 40% of ensembles showing the \nupper-level ridge rebounding with a return of heat. The other \n60% show degrees of troughing, which would keep temperatures \ncooler into early next week. /vmt",
